          "anchor_excerpt": "Liu Wansu was born in northern China under Song Huizong (r. 1100-1126), the famous painter-emperor whose sumptuous reign ended when he lost half of his empire to northern invaders, the Jurchens who founded the Jin dynasty in 1115. $ ^{1} $ From his mid-twenties, Liu pored through Wang Bing's annotated version of the Basic Questions and became engrossed in the complexity of its immensely influential cosmological chapters. $ ^{2} $ To those tenacious enough to decipher them, these chapters explained the convoluted workings of the “five circulatory phases and six seasonal influences” (wuyun liuqi 五運六氣; often referred to as yunqi) as they applied to medicine. $ ^{3} $ The “five circulatory phases” were Wood, Fire, Earth, Metal, and Water, the Five Agents familiar to those who have studied any aspect of Chinese thought. The six types of climatic Qi were Wind 風, Heat 熱, Dampness 濕, Fire 火 (or Summer Heat 署), Dryness 燥, and Cold 寒."
